      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE OF REVIEW",
          "content": "To provide up-to-date information on outcomes and adverse events associated with the use of recombinant human growth hormone (rhGH). We will focus on patients with Prader Willi Syndrome and Idiopathic Short Stature. We will also discuss recent reports on long-term adverse events from the European database."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RECENT FINDINGS",
          "content": "Prader Willi Syndrome is associated with hypogonadism, which does not appear to be affected by treatment with rhGH. However, there is new evidence that treatment may improve cognition. For patients with Idiopathic Short Stature, the gain in near adult height with treatment with rhGH appears to be 3-4\u200acm. Although a recent analysis of this group shows that certain patient characteristics may help identify those most likely to have a good response to treatment. Additionally, the safety and appropriateness of growth hormone treatments in Europe study released preliminary data from patients treated in two separate locations that showed conflicting information on risk of mortality from treatment with rhGH."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "SUMMARY",
          "content": "Although we will continue to receive new information regarding the safety and effects of treatment with rhGH, it is important to discuss the risks and benefits with our patients. Additionally, it is incumbent on us to help guide the treatment to those most likely to benefit."
        }
      ]},
